{
"ed_a": "Answer-letter preference. The model may prefer some option letters (positions) regardless of content; measure this on practice questions (predicted vs true letter counts, and the letter probabilities on questions it gets wrong). If it is there, find what writes the position preference into the answer position (heads, MLP neurons, a constant bias direction, or a dependence on where each option sits in the prompt) and remove it in a content-preserving way. A uniform prior shift tuned to 10 options may not transfer to 4-option GPQA, so prefer fixing the mechanism.",
"ed_b": "Near misses and late-layer loss. When the model is wrong, check how often the correct letter is its second choice. Use the logit lens (residual stream after each layer, through the final norm and the tied output layer, restricted to the option letters) to find whether the correct answer leads at some middle layer and is lost later, and which late components (MLP neurons, attention heads) overwrite it.",
"claude": "How the answer is routed from the option text to the answer position: which attention heads at the last token attend to the option lines, whether they select by content or by position, and whether some heads systematically move the wrong option."}

{
"ed_a": "The option selection of layer 22 head 12 (the answer-routing head found in generation 1). Use read passes with attn_last=[22] on rendered practice questions to see which option spans head 12 attends to from the answer position, and whether non-content factors steer it (option position or recency, through the partial rotary dimensions of the full-attention layer; letter identity; option length). If such a factor biases it, repair it in head 12's query/key weights.",
"ed_b": "The correctness belief at the option positions upstream of layer 22. Compare residual streams at the end of each option for correct and incorrect options, find the layer and components where a correct-vs-incorrect signal is formed, and check whether head 12 of layer 22 reads it through its keys. If the signal exists but is read weakly or is diluted, strengthen the path.",
"claude": "Near misses: questions where the correct letter is ranked second. Decompose the correct-minus-chosen margin by component and find what systematically flips the top two."}

{
"ed_a": "Unused option-text knowledge. In generation 2, the likelihood of each option text after the question (a cloze score) picked the gold option on 25% of the questions the letter protocol gets wrong. Find where the model represents an option text as plausible at the option tokens, compare that with the option-end correctness belief that layer 22 head 12 reads, and look for a targeted edit that routes the missing knowledge into that belief. Screen by paired gold rank, not only accuracy.",
"ed_b": "The content side of layer 22 head 12. Layer 22 has no positional rotation, so its attention scores are exact query-key dot products. Find which key features at option tokens carry the correct-option signal, and which question features the answer-position query uses to match them. Then strengthen the content match in head 12 query/key weights without reintroducing letter or position bias. Screen by paired gold rank.",
"claude": "The belief-forming writers in layers 8-14 (MLP, conv and attention outputs at the option-end tokens). Find components whose contribution separates correct from incorrect options in a way that generalises (cross-validated), and strengthen them. Screen by paired gold rank, not by confidence."}
